SELinux (Security-Enhanced Linux)
acts like an extra, hyper-strict security guard built directly into your Linux server's kernel. 
Even if a hacker or a broken application somehow gains root (admin) privileges, SELinux prevents them from damaging your system or accessing unauthorized files.

Standard Permissions vs. SELinux
Traditional Linux Permissions (DAC): You control access using Owner, Group, and Others (rwx). 
If a hacker compromises an application running as root, they instantly gain access to the entire system.
SELinux (MAC - Mandatory Access Control): Every single file, directory, process, and port is given a specific security label (or context). 
A process can only access a file if its security label explicitly matches the file's label. Even if a process runs as root, SELinux will block it if the labels don't match.

The Three Operational Modes:
Enforcing: The default and safest state. Rules are strictly applied, and any unauthorized access is blocked and logged.
Permissive: The system does not block anything, but it logs violations. This mode is used for troubleshooting or testing a new app.
Disabled: Completely turned off. Security features are inactive.

Essential commands to know: 
Check the current state: getenforce or sestatus
Switch temporarily to Permissive mode: sudo setenforce 0
Switch temporarily back to Enforcing mode: sudo setenforce 1
View the security labels on files: ls -Z

--------------------example commands--------------------
To configure SELinux persistently, use the /etc/selinux/config file

ps axZ
systemctl start httpd
ps -ZC httpd
ls -Z /var/www
getenforce
setenforce 0
getenforce
setenforce Enforcing
getenforce
touch /tmp/file1 /tmp/file2
ls -Z /tmp/file*
ls -Zd /var/www/html/
ls -Z /var/www/html/index.html
mv /tmp/file1 /var/www/html/
cp /tmp/file2 /var/www/html/
ls -Z /var/www/html/file*
mkdir /virtual
ls -Zd /virtual
chcon -t httpd_sys_content_t /virtual
ls -Zd /virtual
restorecon -v /virtual
ls -Zd /virtual
ls -Z /var/www/html/file*
semanage fcontext -l
restorecon -Rv /var/www/
mkdir /virtual
touch /virtual/index.html
touch /virtual/index.html
ls -Z /virtual/
semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t '/virtual(/.*)?'
restorecon -RFvv /virtual
ls -Zd /virtual/
ls -Z /virtual/
semanage fcontext -l -C

--------------------------------------------------------
Basic File Context Operations
-a, --add	Add a record of the specified object type.
-d, --delete	Delete a record of the specified object type.
-l, --list	List records of the specified object type.




